<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shall We Gather at the River?</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Shall We Gather at the River?&quot;, or simply &quot;At the River&quot;, are the popular names of a Christian hymn first titled &quot;Beautiful River&quot; and later &quot;Hanson Place&quot;. Its words and music were written in 1864 by the American poet and gospel composer Robert Lowry (1826–1899), a Baptist minister.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The hymn was first titled Beautiful River and later Hanson Place, after the original Hanson Place Baptist Church in Brooklyn, where Lowry sometimes served as minister; the building now houses another denomination. The music is in the key of D and in an 8.7.8.7 metre with refrain. The hymn is in the public domain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The words look forward to the Christian hope of restoration and reward. They draw on Revelation 22:1-2, with its crystal-clear river of the water of life flowing from the throne of heaven, shown by an angel of God, and recall Acts 16:13, where Paul found women gathered by the river outside the city gates. The chorus runs: Yes, we'll gather at the river, the beautiful, the beautiful river; gather with the saints at the river that flows by the throne of God.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Charles Ives arranged the hymn as At the River, and later settings appear in Aaron Copland's Old American Songs (1952), Anton Plate's At the River (2003) and David Maslanka's Symphony No. 9 (2011). It was sung at the funeral of the Supreme Court Justice William O. Douglas in 1980, and Patti Page, Burl Ives and Willie Nelson recorded it.</p><p>It was one of the director John Ford's favourite hymns, heard in Stagecoach (1939), My Darling Clementine (1946), Wagon Master (1950), twice in The Searchers (1956) and other films. Sam Peckinpah used it in Major Dundee (1965) and in The Wild Bunch (1969) as ironic counterpoint to a massacre, and it also features in David Lean's Hobson's Choice (1954) and Richard Brooks's Elmer Gantry (1960).</p><p>The tune travelled abroad. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> the 1876 hymn O, hur saligt att få vandra, sung to it, became one of the most popular songs of the revivalist movement. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> it became the Christmas carol Welchen Jubel, welche Freude with words by Ernst Gebhardt, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> it was set in 1937 to the enka song Tabakoya no Musume. The University of Antioquia in Medellin uses the melody in its official song.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shall We Gather at the River?” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
